(function(root){const notes={"sign": {"path": "hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md", "text": "# First-move ablation: the upper-tail reduction\n\n## Protocol fixed before model results\n\nRemove reference action `sign`, from `state:start`: the reduction of the two-sided\nHanson–Wright goal to an upper-tail bound, followed by applying it to −A.\nThe proposed pilot starts from the theorem and assumptions and prohibits this\nstrategy throughout the argument, including signed-tail/cosh disguises. It permits\nabsolute moments, independent copies, conditioning and lower-level scalar/linear\nand independent-sum toolkit results. The complete machine-readable restriction\nand prompts are in `tools/proof_explorer/ablation_study.py`.\n\nTwo fresh attempts per condition (unrestricted and restricted); model configured\nby the existing project; medium reasoning; 6,500 completion-token cap per search\ncall; at most two search calls per trial. A separate review call has a 4,500 cap.\nOne revision after review is allowed. Stop on acceptance; at most 16 calls across\nall trials. Review is by the same model in a separate context, not formal checking.\nNo reference proof, other trial, or the local derivation below enters the requests.\nFull requests, public responses, provider token usage and call duration are logged.\nReview costs are separated from search costs. A failed finite budget is censored,\nnot infinite difficulty. This is a small descriptive pilot, not a statistical\ncriticality estimate or an incremental student/professor run.\n\nThe API usage fields and completion-token limit follow the\n[official API documentation](https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/chat/subresources/completions/methods/create).\nThe completion cap includes reasoning tokens as well as visible output.\n\n## Local mathematical reconstruction, separate from the empirical pilot\n\nThis derivation was constructed by the coding assistant while API execution was\nawaiting approval. It is not a fresh model-search sample, has no measured search\ncost, and must not be counted in the empirical success rate.\n\nThere is a route around the sign reduction: prove an absolute-moment estimate and\napply Markov directly to |Q|ᵖ. No upper-tail theorem for Q or −Q is needed.\n\nWrite F=‖A‖F, O=‖A‖ and Q=D+S, where\n\n    D = Σᵢ aᵢᵢ(Xᵢ²−E Xᵢ²),    S = Σᵢ≠ⱼ aᵢⱼ Xᵢ Xⱼ.\n\nFor even p≥2, the independent centered subexponential-sum moment inequality gives\n\n    ‖D‖p ≤ C K²(√p F + p O).\n\nToolkit dependency: for independent centered subexponential Yi and fixed weights\nbi, ‖Σ bi Yi‖p ≤ C(√p (Σ bi²‖Yi‖ψ₁²)¹ᐟ² + p max |bi|‖Yi‖ψ₁).\nHere Yi=Xi²−E Xi² has ψ₁ norm at most CK². The inequalities\nΣ aᵢᵢ²≤F² and max|aᵢᵢ|≤O check the required scales. This is an\nindependent-sum moment result, not a quadratic-form concentration theorem.\n\nTo check that this does not conceal a signed-tail reduction, it can be proved\ndirectly by expanding even powers. Set Zi=aᵢᵢYi, choose Mi=C K²|aᵢᵢ| so that\nE|Zi|ᵏ≤k! Miᵏ for k≥2, and put σ²=ΣMi², b=max Mi. Centering removes terms\nwith exponent one. Independence and the multinomial expansion give the following\ncoefficient-wise bound of formal power series, for p=2m:\n\n    E|Σ Zi|²ᵐ / (2m)! ≤ [z²ᵐ] ∏ᵢ (1 + Σₖ≥₂ Miᵏ zᵏ)\n                       ≤ [z²ᵐ] exp(σ²z²/(1−bz)).\n\nAll coefficients in the upper bound are nonnegative. For any 0<r<1/b, the last\ncoefficient is at most r⁻²ᵐ exp(σ²r²/(1−br)). Take\nr=min{√m/(2σ),1/(2b)}. The exponent is at most m/2, and (2m)!¹ᐟ²ᵐ≤2m.\nThus ‖ΣZi‖₂ₘ≤C(√m σ+mb), as needed. Zero coefficients give the immediate\ndegenerate case. This uses coefficient estimates for even moments, not a tail\nestimate for D or −D; it introduces no signed quadratic-form MGF.\n\nLet δi be independent Bernoulli(1/2) selectors, independent of X, and define\nSδ=Σᵢ≠ⱼ δi(1−δj)aᵢⱼXiXj. Since Eδ Sδ=S/4, Jensen gives\n\n    ‖S‖p ≤ 4(Eδ E|Sδ|ᵖ)¹ᐟᵖ.\n\nFor fixed Λ={i:δi=1}, Sδ=XΛᵀB XΛᶜ with B=AΛ,Λᶜ. The two vectors are\nindependent, even for nonsymmetric A. Conditional scalar linear-form moments yield\n\n    ‖Sδ‖p ≤ CK√p · (E‖B XΛᶜ‖₂ᵖ)¹ᐟᵖ.\n\nThe necessary vector bound follows without a signed quadratic MGF. Let g be an\nindependent standard Gaussian vector in the output space of B and ap=‖g₁‖p≈√p. Gaussian\nprojection followed by conditioning on g gives\n\n    ap (E‖BY‖₂ᵖ)¹ᐟᵖ = (E|⟨g,BY⟩|ᵖ)¹ᐟᵖ\n                          ≤ CK√p (E‖Bᵀg‖₂ᵖ)¹ᐟᵖ.\n\nThe standard Gaussian Lipschitz moment inequality implies\n\n    (E‖Bᵀg‖₂ᵖ)¹ᐟᵖ ≤ E‖Bᵀg‖₂ + C√p‖B‖\n                       ≤ ‖B‖F + C√p‖B‖.\n\nThis follows from Theorem 5.2.3 (Gaussian concentration) and the subgaussian\nmoment characterization in Proposition 2.6.1 of the supplied HDP-2 book.\nIts hypotheses hold because g is standard Gaussian and g↦‖Bᵀg‖₂ is\n‖B‖-Lipschitz. Jensen bounds its mean by ‖B‖F. This is a Gaussian Lipschitz\ntoolkit dependency, not Hanson–Wright in disguise. Dividing by ap yields\n\n    (E‖BY‖₂ᵖ)¹ᐟᵖ ≤ CK(‖B‖F + √p‖B‖).\n\nCoordinate projections give ‖B‖F≤F and ‖B‖≤O, so\n\n    ‖S‖p ≤ CK²(√p F + p O),\n    ‖Q‖p ≤ C₀K²(√p F + p O).\n\nFor q=min{t²/(K⁴F²),t/(K²O)}, choose a universal α>0 with\nC₀(√α+α)≤1/e. If αq≥4, take the even integer p=2⌊αq/2⌋.\nThen αq/2≤p≤αq. Apply Markov directly:\n\n    P(|Q|≥t) ≤ (‖Q‖p/t)ᵖ ≤ exp(−p) ≤ exp(−αq/2).\n\nFor αq<4 use P≤1. Taking c≤α log(2)/4 gives the requested uniform bound\nP(|Q|≥t)≤2exp(−cq) in both regimes. At t=0 the bound is immediate. If A=0\nor K=0, Q=0 almost surely and positive-threshold probabilities vanish.\n\n### What this establishes and what it does not\n\nThe sign/upper-tail reduction is mathematically replaceable relative to the\nstated toolkit. The replacement route bypasses the upper-tail checkpoint and\nreaches the final two-sided theorem directly. It carries additional explicit\nmoment and Gaussian-Lipschitz dependencies. This derivation alone says nothing\nabout how easily a fresh search finds the route, how much it costs relative to a\nbaseline, or whether the first move is empirically critical for a given model.\n\n\n## Companion notes\n\nSee [the all-step ablation index](hanson_wright_ablation_notes.md) for the other\n13 reference moves, their precise strategy bans, and worked or unresolved alternatives.\n"}, "common": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/00-common.md", "text": "# Shared conventions and verified calculations for the ablation notes\n\nThese notes extend the [first-step reconstruction](../hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md).\nThey are mathematical analyses written locally, not independent search trials,\nformal certificates, or measurements of difficulty. All constants below are\nuniversal and may change from line to line. No API experiment was executed for\nthese notes. None of the unsuccessful candidate arguments establishes infinite\nsearch difficulty.\n\n## What is held fixed\n\nThe theorem concerns a fixed real matrix A and independent, mean-zero coordinates\nwith ‖Xᵢ‖ψ₂ ≤ K. Put\n\n    Q = XᵀAX − E XᵀAX, F = ‖A‖F, O = ‖A‖,\n    V = K⁴F², L = K²O, q(t) = min{t²/V, t/L}.\n\nThe target is P(|Q| ≥ t) ≤ 2 exp(−c q(t)). We initially take t,K,F,O > 0.\nFor t = 0 the probability bound is immediate; if A = 0 or K = 0 then Q = 0\nalmost surely and positive-threshold probabilities vanish.\n\nEvery ablation starts from this theorem and these assumptions. The location of\nan original edge identifies what is banned, not a cache of facts that may be\nreused. A displayed rejoin is legitimate only after its hypotheses and conclusion\nhave been established along the alternative route. A changed constant or domain\nmust be propagated to all subsequent steps.\n\nBans apply throughout the new proof, including the dependencies of quoted\nlemmas. Calling Hanson–Wright, or a theorem that supplies exactly the missing\nquadratic-form estimate, is not an admissible shortcut. Renaming a banned\nargument or importing it behind an opaque theorem also fails the audit.\n\n## Separate conclusions\n\n- **Worked replacement:** a complete route, possibly using another note's fully\n  specified calculation, reaches the theorem under the stated ban and toolkit.\n- **Insufficient candidate:** the displayed argument gives a weaker bound or\n  violates a hypothesis. This diagnoses that candidate, not every possible route.\n- **Unresolved:** a complete admissible route is not provided here.\n- **Ban needs refinement:** forbidding a basic inference or a mathematical\n  consequence of the desired theorem does not isolate a useful proof strategy.\n\nThere is no numerical discovery-cost estimate. Proof length, n conditioning\nsteps, a lost factor in a norm estimate, and token cost are different quantities.\nA finite written argument does not show that a model can discover it within a\nspecified finite search budget. Historical occurrence counts are also unmeasured.\n\n## M: the existing absolute-moment route\n\nThe first-step note proves, uniformly over even integers p ≥ 2,\n\n    ‖Q‖p ≤ C₀K²(√p F + p O).                         (M)\n\nIts actual dependencies are: Q = D + S; a centered even-power coefficient\nestimate for D; Bernoulli selector decoupling of S; scalar linear-form moments;\nGaussian projection; Gaussian Lipschitz norm concentration; and Minkowski.\nIt avoids signed quadratic-form MGFs, the upper-tail/sign reduction, and matrix\nSVD. It does **not** avoid diagonal/off-diagonal separation, decoupling, all\nGaussian auxiliary variables, or scale-sensitive parameter selection.\n\nA note using M must audit these dependencies against its own ban. It must not\nlabel reuse of M as another independent discovery.\n\nFor completeness, the common closing argument is:\n\n    Choose α > 0 with C₀(√α + α) ≤ e⁻¹.\n    If αq ≥ 4, take p = 2⌊αq/2⌋, so αq/2 ≤ p ≤ αq.\n    P(|Q| ≥ t) ≤ (‖Q‖p/t)ᵖ ≤ e⁻ᵖ ≤ e^(−αq/2).\n\nIf αq < 4, use P ≤ 1 and choose c ≤ α log(2)/4. This establishes the required\nprefactor 2, including small thresholds. The even rounding is necessary because\nM was only proved at even orders.\n\n## E: a centered independent-sum MGF estimate\n\nThis calculation is available only when the ablation permits exponential control\nof the diagonal. It cannot be smuggled into the Bernstein or quadratic-MGF bans.\n\nFor Zᵢ = aᵢᵢ(Xᵢ² − EXᵢ²), choose Mᵢ = CK²|aᵢᵢ| with\nE|Zᵢ|ᵏ ≤ k! Mᵢᵏ for k ≥ 2. For |λ|Mᵢ ≤ 1/2, absolute convergence and centering give\n\n    E exp(λZᵢ) ≤ 1 + Σₖ≥₂ (|λ|Mᵢ)ᵏ\n               ≤ exp(2λ²Mᵢ²).\n\nIndependence therefore gives\n\n    E exp(λD) ≤ exp(Cλ²K⁴Σᵢaᵢᵢ²),  |λ| ≤ c/(K² maxᵢ|aᵢᵢ|).\n\nZero weights require no division. In particular this is ≤ exp(CVλ²) for\n|λ| ≤ c/L. This is a reconstruction of the usual exponential independent-sum\nmechanism, not a new strategy merely because it avoids naming Bernstein.\n\n## H: combine MGFs before taking a tail\n\nSuppose D and S have individually established bounds\n\n    E exp(uD) ≤ exp(C_D V u²),  |u| ≤ a_D/L,\n    E exp(uS) ≤ exp(C_S V u²),  |u| ≤ a_S/L.\n\nThey need not be independent. Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\n    E exp(λQ) ≤ (E exp(2λD) E exp(2λS))^(1/2)\n               ≤ exp(2(C_D+C_S)Vλ²),\n    |λ| ≤ min(a_D,a_S)/(2L).\n\nThe factor 2 shrinks the domain and increases the exponent constant; retaining\nonly the formula and dropping the domain would be invalid. Write the resulting\nconstants as C_* and a_*. For each sign, choose\n\n    λ = min{t/(2C_*V), a_*/(2L)}.\n\nThen C_*Vλ² ≤ λt/2 and the corresponding tail is at most\nexp[−c min(t²/V,t/L)]. The two signs give prefactor 2 directly. No event split\nbetween D and S is used. This route still uses their algebraic separation,\nquadratic MGFs, Chernoff tuning, and the two-sign assembly.\n\n## G: Gaussian quadratic estimate with explicit assumptions\n\nFor symmetric B with tr B = 0 and independent standard Gaussian coordinates g,\nlet μᵢ be its eigenvalues. For each coordinate the defining Gaussian integral\n∫ exp[−(1−2λσ²μᵢ)x²/2] dx / √(2π) equals (1−2λσ²μᵢ)^(−1/2). If |2λσ²|‖B‖ ≤ 1/2, a Gaussian integral gives\n\n    log E exp(λσ²gᵀBg)\n      = −(1/2) Σᵢ log(1 − 2λσ²μᵢ)\n      = (1/2) Σᵢ [−log(1 − zᵢ) − zᵢ],   zᵢ = 2λσ²μᵢ.\n\nThe linear terms cancel because tr B = 0. The absolutely convergent expansion\nΣₖ≥₂ zᵏ/k implies −log(1−z)−z ≤ C z² for |z| ≤ 1/2. Consequently\n\n    E exp(λσ²gᵀBg) ≤ exp(Cλ²σ⁴‖B‖F²),\n    |λ| ≤ c/(σ²‖B‖).\n\nThis calculation uses Gaussian diagonalization and the Gaussian-square integral.\nIt is forbidden in the rotation and exact-Gaussian-MGF ablations below. Trace\ncancellation is essential; the same estimate cannot be asserted for an arbitrary\nuncentered PSD quadratic form.\n\n## Dependency and interpretation checks\n\nThe original reference graph's `gaussian` and `replace` moves propose and carry\nout essentially the same Gaussian MGF comparison. Their shared strategy ban\nmust be reported as one family when interpreting results. Likewise, `meet`\npackages several preceding estimates, and `finish` packages endpoint assembly.\nAblating those graph nodes does not automatically create new independent\nmathematical bottlenecks.\n\nThe available sources help identify permissible techniques, not the original\nauthor's private motivations or how frequently a method was tried. The\n[Rudelson–Vershynin paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.2872) is a reference for the\nclassical theorem and proof family. The separate decoupling note cites a more\nrecent, different comparison argument. Detailed mathematical claims in these\nnotes are accompanied by their calculations and restrictions.\n"}, "expand": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/02-expand.md", "text": "# Step 2 — `expand`: separate diagonal and off-diagonal terms\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe original move writes Q = D + S, using independence and centering to remove\ncross terms from E XᵀAX. It exposes an independent sum of squares and a dependent\ncross-term sum.\n\nFor a meaningful structural ablation, forbid this diagonal/off-diagonal\nseparation **anywhere in the new proof**, including hollow-matrix notation,\nseparate lemmas for those two contributions, or a coordinate induction that\nquietly analyzes precisely the same two pieces. Do not prohibit algebra or the\nuse of independence itself. Those would be much broader restrictions.\n\nThe existing moment proof is inadmissible: its first substantive operation is\nexactly Q = D + S.\n\n## Alternative tried analytically: whole-form symmetrization\n\nReplace A by B = (A + Aᵀ)/2. This preserves the quadratic form and gives\n‖B‖ ≤ O and ‖B‖F ≤ F without separating the diagonal. Let X′ be an independent\ncopy. Conditional Jensen and polarization yield, for p ≥ 2,\n\n    ‖Q‖p ≤ ‖XᵀBX − X′ᵀBX′‖p\n          = ‖(X − X′)ᵀB(X + X′)‖p.\n\nThis is a valid change of representation under the stated ban. A direct\nCauchy–Schwarz bound gives\n\n    ‖Q‖p ≤ O · ‖X − X′‖L₂ₚ(ℓ₂) · ‖X + X′‖L₂ₚ(ℓ₂)\n          ≤ CK² O(n + p).\n\nThe last inequality uses the vector norm moment bound from the first-step note,\napplied to the identity matrix. It follows from scalar linear-form moments and\nGaussian projection/Lipschitz control; it does not use a D/S split of Q.\n\n## Why this does not close the required theorem\n\nThe target moment scale is K²(√p F + p O). For A = I, the displayed alternative\nhas order K²(n+p), whereas the target is K²(√pn+p). At fixed p and growing n,\nthe available bound is too coarse. This is a loss in the **proved bound**, not a\nmeasurement of computational cost or a proof that every polarized route fails.\n\nA tempting next line treats X − X′ and X + X′ as independent. That is false for\ngeneral subgaussian coordinates. For independent Rademacher Xᵢ,Xᵢ′, their sum\nand difference multiply to zero, and each is nonzero with positive probability;\nthey are not independent. Independence holds in the Gaussian special case, which\nis not enough for the stated theorem.\n\nAn admissible continuation would need a sharp bound for this dependent bilinear\nexpression without reintroducing diagonal/off-diagonal separation. That bound\nhas not been supplied here. Calling Hanson–Wright for it would be circular.\n\n## Graph representation, cost, and conclusion\n\n    original assumptions\n      → symmetrize the matrix\n      → independent-copy centering and polarization\n      → dependent bilinear moment obligation\n      → coarse O(n+p) estimate / sharp continuation unresolved\n\nThere is no justified rejoin or theorem closure. Record the coarse candidate as\n**insufficient for the required norm scales**, with the broader ablation\n**unresolved**. It is not an infinite-difficulty branch. A restriction against\nonly the literal coordinate expansion, while allowing the same D/S decomposition\nunder different notation, would test presentation rather than this strategy.\n\nSearch cost and historical frequency are unmeasured. See the [shared protocol](00-common.md).\n"}, "split": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/03-split.md", "text": "# Step 3 — `split`: allocate t/2 to two tail events\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe original edge bounds P(D+S ≥ t) by P(D ≥ t/2)+P(S ≥ t/2). Here the ablated\nstrategy is **splitting a deviation event into diagonal and off-diagonal tail\nevents and allocating thresholds to them**, including unequal allocations.\nBan that operation throughout the proof.\n\nAlgebraically writing Q = D + S remains allowed. This makes the experiment\ndistinct from the stronger structural ban in [step 2](02-expand.md). If “give\nthe terms different jobs” is intended to prohibit *any* separate treatment of D\nand S, the alternative below is inadmissible and step 2's unresolved conclusion\napplies instead. This scope must be fixed before a search trial.\n\n## Worked alternative: combine transforms before taking a tail\n\nStart again from the theorem. Establish the diagonal MGF by calculation E in\n[the shared notes](00-common.md). Establish the off-diagonal MGF through the\npermitted decoupling, Gaussian replacement and Gaussian computation route:\n\n    E exp(uD) ≤ exp(C_D V u²), |u| ≤ a_D/L,\n    E exp(uS) ≤ exp(C_S V u²), |u| ≤ a_S/L.\n\nFor negative u use the same permitted estimates with −A. Now apply\nCauchy–Schwarz, not a decomposition of the tail event:\n\n    E exp(λQ) ≤ √(E exp(2λD) E exp(2λS))\n               ≤ exp(2(C_D+C_S)Vλ²),\n    |λ| ≤ min(a_D,a_S)/(2L).\n\nCalculation H in the shared notes then gives the complete two-sided theorem.\nD and S need not be independent. The domain shrinkage by 2 is part of the\nargument, not a dispensable constant annotation.\n\nA second admissible route is the [absolute-moment proof](../hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md):\ncombine D and S by Minkowski and apply Markov to |Q|ᵖ. It also avoids splitting\nthe D/S tail event, but still treats the algebraic pieces separately.\n\n## Other natural attempt and its failure\n\nWriting E exp(λ(D+S)) = E exp(λD) E exp(λS) would be shorter, but D and S share\ncoordinates. No such independence is assumed. The Schwarz inequality above is\nwhat makes the transformed assembly valid without that false factorization.\n\n## Rejoin and implications\n\n    assumptions → D/S MGF estimates → whole-Q MGF\n                → whole-Q Chernoff bound → two-sided theorem\n\nThe route bypasses the two tail checkpoints. It reaches the original endpoint,\nrather than pretending to recreate the old `split` state. The existing diagonal\ntail result alone is not enough for the Schwarz step; the diagonal MGF must be\nproved explicitly.\n\n**Conclusion:** worked replacement under the event-splitting ban. Constants\nchange but the Frobenius/operator norm scales are retained. No discovery-cost\ncomparison is available. This does not show that the stronger diagonal/off-diagonal\nstructural split is replaceable.\n"}, "bernstein": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/04-bernstein.md", "text": "# Step 4 — `bernstein`: close the diagonal with an exponential sum bound\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe original proof applies Bernstein to independent centered subexponential\nsummands Zᵢ = aᵢᵢ(Xᵢ² − EXᵢ²).\n\nBan invoking Bernstein for this diagonal sum **and** reconstructing its usual\nexponential-MGF/Chernoff proof for D under another name. Permit even-power moment\ncalculations and absolute Markov. A ban on the resulting two-scale inequality\nitself would forbid a necessary special case of the target theorem when A is\ndiagonal, so it would not isolate a proof technique.\n\n## Worked alternative: positive coefficient estimates\n\nThe complete calculation is in the [first-step note](../hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md).\nIts relevant steps are reproduced here to make the replacement explicit.\nChoose Mᵢ = CK²|aᵢᵢ| with E|Zᵢ|ᵏ ≤ k!Mᵢᵏ for k ≥ 2, and set\nσ² = ΣMᵢ², b = max Mᵢ. Centering kills singleton factors in an even-power expansion:\n\n    E|ΣZᵢ|²ᵐ/(2m)!\n      ≤ [z²ᵐ] ∏ᵢ(1 + Σₖ≥₂ Mᵢᵏzᵏ)\n      ≤ [z²ᵐ] exp(σ²z²/(1−bz)).\n\nThis is a coefficientwise majorant of a formal series, not E exp(λD). Its\ncoefficients are nonnegative. Evaluate at\n\n    r = min{√m/(2σ), 1/(2b)}.\n\nThe coefficient is at most r⁻²ᵐ exp(m/2), and ((2m)!)¹ᐟ²ᵐ ≤ 2m. Thus\n\n    ‖D‖₂ₘ ≤ C(√m σ + mb) ≤ CK²(√p F + p O), p = 2m.\n\nIf b = 0, D = 0 and no radius is needed. Follow the existing off-diagonal moment\nroute, use Minkowski, and close the full theorem by the even-order Markov\nargument M in [the shared notes](00-common.md). No diagonal exponential tail or\nMGF has been used.\n\n## A cheaper-looking attempt that loses the theorem's scale\n\nTriangle inequality term by term gives\n\n    ‖D‖p ≤ Σᵢ |aᵢᵢ| ‖Xᵢ²−EXᵢ²‖p\n          ≤ CK²p Σᵢ|aᵢᵢ|.\n\nFor A = I this is CK²pn rather than CK²(√pn+p). It discards the cancellation\nfrom centering. The coefficient argument retains that cancellation through the\nabsence of exponent-one terms.\n\n## Rejoin, cost, and conclusion\n\nThe preferred branch reaches the diagonal **moment** goal, then the full moment\ngoal and final theorem. It does not directly rejoin the original diagonal-tail\nstate unless a separate moment-to-tail inference is included.\n\n**Conclusion:** worked replacement for the specified exponential independent-sum\nstrategy. It adds coefficient bookkeeping and an even-moment parameter choice.\nThat extra mathematical work is visible, but its discovery cost is unmeasured.\nIf the ban is broadened to all centered independent-sum moment aggregation, this\nargument no longer qualifies; it should not be counted under that broader ban.\n"}, "exponential": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/05-exponential.md", "text": "# Step 5 — `exponential`: turn a quadratic tail into an MGF goal\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe reference proof applies exponential Markov to S and asks for\nE exp(λS) ≤ exp(CVλ²) on a cutoff interval.\n\nBan the entire quadratic-MGF/Chernoff strategy for Q, D, S and their transformed\nquadratic or bilinear substitutes. This includes cosh or signed-MGF disguises\nand quoting a quadratic MGF lemma that performs the omitted work internally.\nScalar/linear subgaussian toolkit results and Gaussian norm concentration remain\navailable; an auxiliary norm's Lipschitz concentration is not a quadratic-form\nMGF bound.\n\n## Worked alternative: control |Q| by its even moments\n\nUse the [first-step moment construction](../hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md),\nwith its dependencies explicitly retained:\n\n    Q = D + S;\n    ‖D‖p ≤ CK²(√p F + p O)              [even-power coefficients];\n    ‖S‖p ≤ CK²(√p F + p O)              [selectors and vector moments];\n    ‖Q‖p ≤ C₀K²(√p F + p O)            [Minkowski].\n\nThe off-diagonal estimate follows from\n\n    ‖S‖p ≤ 4‖XΛᵀB XΛᶜ‖Lp(δ,X),\n    ‖XΛᵀB Y‖p ≤ CK√p ‖BY‖Lp(ℓ₂),\n    ‖BY‖Lp(ℓ₂) ≤ CK(‖B‖F + √p‖B‖).\n\nThe last line uses a Gaussian projection and the Lipschitz moment bound for\n‖Bᵀg‖₂, not a Gaussian bilinear MGF. Uniform bounds ‖B‖F ≤ F and ‖B‖ ≤ O\nallow averaging over the selectors. The diagonal's formal coefficient majorant\nis likewise not a random quadratic exponential moment.\n\nApply Markov to |Q|ᵖ with the even p and small-q handling specified in calculation\nM of [the shared notes](00-common.md). This reaches the full two-sided theorem.\n\n## Why second moments alone do not replace Chernoff\n\nAt p = 2 the construction gives E Q² ≤ CK⁴F²: O ≤ F absorbs the second term.\nChebyshev then gives only P(|Q| ≥ t) ≤ CK⁴F²/t². This is a valid polynomial tail,\nbut it does not establish the required exponential tail. A family of increasing\nmoment orders is doing essential work in the successful replacement.\n\n## Graph, cost, and conclusion\n\n    quadratic tail goal → all-even-moment goal\n       → diagonal/off-diagonal moment branches → full moment bound\n       → absolute Markov with even rounding → theorem\n\nThe route bypasses the original MGF checkpoint completely. It must not be drawn\nas having established that checkpoint along the way.\n\n**Conclusion:** worked replacement. It is the same substantive alternative as\nthe first-step construction, admissible under a different ban; it is not a new\nindependent search success. Search cost, probability of rediscovery, and any\nrelative advantage over the MGF route remain unmeasured.\n"}, "linear": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/06-linear.md", "text": "# Step 6 — `linear`: multiply scalar MGFs to control a linear form\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe reference proof factors E exp(λΣvᵢXᵢ) by independence and multiplies scalar\nMGF bounds. Ban this **linear-MGF factorization/comparison strategy** throughout\nthe new proof, including importing its linear-form bound under an opaque name.\nAllow independence in moment expansions, symmetrization, and Gaussian moments.\n\nForbidding *any* sharp linear-form control would be a much stronger experiment.\nIt would also forbid a consequence of the target theorem: apply Hanson–Wright\nto vvᵀ to control (vᵀX)² about its mean and use\nE(vᵀX)² ≤ CK²‖v‖₂² to obtain a subgaussian large-deviation bound for vᵀX.\nThe ban here concerns the derivation, not that necessary kind of conclusion.\n\n## Worked alternative: compare even moments after symmetrization\n\nLet X′ be an independent copy and Zᵢ = Xᵢ−Xᵢ′. Conditional Jensen gives\n\n    E|ΣvᵢXᵢ|²ᵐ ≤ E|ΣvᵢZᵢ|²ᵐ.\n\nThe Zᵢ are independent and symmetric. Their subgaussian moment bounds and scalar\nGaussian moment lower bounds imply, with one universal a = CK, for every k ≥ 1,\n\n    E Zᵢ²ᵏ ≤ a²ᵏ E gᵢ²ᵏ.\n\nExpand the even power. Any term with an odd exponent has expectation zero.\nEvery remaining coefficient contains only even powers of the real vᵢ and is\nnonnegative. Termwise comparison therefore gives\n\n    E|ΣvᵢZᵢ|²ᵐ ≤ E|aΣvᵢgᵢ|²ᵐ,\n    ‖ΣvᵢXᵢ‖p ≤ CK√p ‖v‖₂, p = 2m.\n\nHere Σvᵢgᵢ is Gaussian with variance ‖v‖₂²; this elementary Gaussian fact can be\nestablished without multiplying the **non-Gaussian linear MGFs** under ablation.\nThe uniform constant follows from ‖Zᵢ‖₂ₖ ≤ CK√k and ‖gᵢ‖₂ₖ ≥ c√k.\n\nUse this lemma at both conditional linear-form calls in the first-step moment\nproof. In each call the coefficients are independent of the coordinates being\naveraged, so conditioning makes them deterministic. Gaussian Lipschitz\nconcentration supplies the remaining vector-norm estimate; the theorem closes\nby absolute Markov. Do not recover a linear MGF from these moments and then\nreuse the banned original comparison chain.\n\n## Why naive norm inequalities are insufficient\n\nTriangle inequality yields CK√p‖v‖₁, with a possible √n loss. Applying\nRademacher Khintchine and then bounding the random coefficient norm crudely\ncan instead yield CKp‖v‖₂, losing √p. The termwise symmetric moment comparison\nkeeps the optimal √p dependence.\n\n## Rejoin, cost, and conclusion\n\nThis branch supplies a linear **moment** lemma and feeds the whole moment proof.\nIt does not recreate the reference `linear` MGF fact verbatim. It permits a full\nclosure under the stated mechanism ban, with additional symmetrization and\neven-moment bookkeeping. Independence and Gaussian moment comparison remain\nexplicit dependencies. Discovery cost is unmeasured; banning all linear-form\nestimates or all independence-based factorization would invalidate this route.\n"}, "decouple": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/07-decouple.md", "text": "# Step 7 — `decouple`: replace dependent cross terms by independent blocks\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe reference route introduces an independent copy and bounds the cross-term\nMGF by the MGF of XᵀAX′. The strategy ban covers independent-copy bilinear\ncomparisons, Bernoulli selector partitions, random cuts, and equivalent\nU-statistic decoupling, wherever they occur in the new proof.\n\nIn particular, the first-step moment construction is **not** admissible: its\nselector step is still decoupling. Merely changing the observable from an\nexponential to an absolute power does not remove that strategy.\n\n## Worked alternative: replace individual coordinates in a hollow form\n\n[Ziemann's 2025 proof, §2](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.00881v1#S2) provides a\ncoordinate-replacement alternative to convex decoupling. The following adapts\nthat comparison to our independent, not necessarily identically distributed\ncoordinates; only a common scalar MGF bound is needed.\n\n### Provenance and what this ablation establishes\n\nThis alternative was found online in Ingvar Ziemann's\n[An Elementary Proof of The Hanson-Wright Inequality, arXiv:2509.00881v1 (2025), §2](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.00881v1#S2).\nThe paper replaces convex decoupling with successive conditional Gaussian\ncomparisons. Our contribution here is adapting and checking that published\nargument against this ablation's assumptions and strategy ban.\n\nRecord this as **a worked, literature-assisted replacement**, not an independent\ndiscovery by the search agents. It demonstrates that the forbidden decoupling\nstrategy is mathematically replaceable with this source available. It does not\nestablish that a first attempter would find the replacement, that it has equal\nor lower discovery cost, or that decoupling is unimportant to the original proof.\nThe n coordinate comparisons count proof-construction steps, not the effort\nneeded to invent the method. Retrieval, adaptation, verification, and independent\ndiscovery are different kinds of work; their costs have not been measured here.\nIn any future source-restricted search study, this result must be labeled as\nsource-assisted and assessed separately from attempts without access to the paper.\n\n### Adapted comparison\n\nSet B = (A+Aᵀ)/2, H = B−diag(B), and σ² = CK² so\nE exp(uXᵢ) ≤ exp(σ²u²/2). In a hybrid vector z containing independent original\nand N(0,σ²) coordinates, condition on all coordinates except i. Because Hᵢᵢ = 0,\n\n    zᵀHz = c + 2zᵢh,   h = Σⱼ≠ᵢ Hᵢⱼzⱼ.\n    Eᵢ exp(λ(c+2Xᵢh)) ≤ exp(λc+2σ²λ²h²)\n                         = E_gᵢ exp(λ(c+2σgᵢh)).\n\nReplace n coordinates successively to obtain\n\n    E exp(λXᵀHX) ≤ E exp(λσ²gᵀHg).\n\nThis uses neither independent copies of the two factors nor a random partition.\nThe zero diagonal makes every conditional exponent linear in the replaced\ncoordinate. The diagonal needs separate treatment.\n\n## Complete the route and audit its assumptions\n\nH is symmetric with trace zero. Calculation G in [the shared notes](00-common.md)\nestablishes\n\n    E exp(λσ²gᵀHg) ≤ exp(Cλ²σ⁴‖H‖F²), |λ| ≤ c/(σ²‖H‖).\n\nThe scale checks are essential:\n\n    ‖B‖F ≤ F, ‖B‖ ≤ O,\n    ‖H‖F ≤ F, ‖H‖ ≤ ‖B‖ + ‖diag(B)‖ ≤ 2O.\n\nControl the centered diagonal by calculation E, combine by calculation H, and\nclose the two-sided theorem. Intermediate conditional expectations are of\nnonnegative functions; iterated integration is valid, and the final finite\nGaussian bound establishes integrability in the stated domain. Gaussian\ncomparison, spectral rotation and exponential tail tuning are allowed here.\n\n## A misleading alternative\n\nRotating X directly to diagonalize A is invalid in general: independent\nsubgaussian coordinates do not stay independent under arbitrary orthogonal\ntransformations. The coordinate replacement makes the rotated vector Gaussian\nfirst. It is a different role for Gaussian comparison, not an assumption that\nX was rotationally invariant.\n\n## Rejoin, cost, and conclusion\n\n    assumptions → symmetric/hollow decomposition\n       → n scalar coordinate comparisons → one Gaussian quadratic form\n       → Gaussian MGF + diagonal MGF → whole-Q MGF → theorem\n\nIt can recover off-diagonal MGF control up to universal constant/domain changes,\nor proceed to the whole-Q endpoint. It never establishes the old decoupled\nbilinear state. **Conclusion:** worked replacement under the decoupling ban.\nThe n comparisons describe an explicit proof construction; they are not measured\nmodel calls or a proof-search complexity estimate. This is a source-assisted\nreconstruction, not a fresh independent rediscovery. It would be inadmissible\nunder an additional Gaussian-comparison ban.\n"}, "gaussian": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/08-gaussian.md", "text": "# Step 8 — `gaussian`: propose a Gaussian bilinear MGF target\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe reference goal reduction matches a subgaussian linear-MGF bound to an exact\nGaussian linear MGF, creating a Gaussian bilinear MGF subproblem.\n\nBan **Gaussian comparison of quadratic/bilinear exponential moments** globally:\nno one-vector or two-vector MGF replacement, hollow-form coordinate MGF\nreplacement, or an imported lemma that performs those comparisons. This is the\nsame strategy family that the next reference action `replace` implements.\n\nGaussian projection of a vector norm and Gaussian Lipschitz norm concentration\nremain allowed. This scope removes the MGF comparison mechanism; it does not\nremove every appearance of a Gaussian variable. The broader “no Gaussian tools\nat all” experiment is recorded separately below.\n\n## Worked alternative: change the target to vector moments\n\nUse the complete absolute-moment route, retaining selector decoupling. For a\nfixed selector block B and independent subvectors U,Y,\n\n    ‖UᵀBY‖p ≤ CK√p ‖BY‖Lp(ℓ₂).\n\nAn independent standard Gaussian g gives an identity for a norm rather than an\nMGF. With a_p = ‖g₁‖p ≍ √p,\n\n    a_p ‖BY‖Lp(ℓ₂) = ‖⟨g,BY⟩‖Lp(g,Y)\n                    ≤ CK√p ‖Bᵀg‖Lp(ℓ₂).\n\nThe two conditionings are different: fix Y for the projection identity and fix\ng for the scalar subgaussian moment bound. Gaussian Lipschitz concentration and\nJensen give\n\n    ‖Bᵀg‖Lp(ℓ₂) ≤ ‖B‖F + C√p‖B‖.\n\nDivide by a_p, return to the conditional bilinear moment, and average the\nselectors. Together with the coefficient proof for D this yields the full\nmoment estimate M and the final theorem. The [first-step note](../hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md)\ncontains the complete assembly and boundary cases.\n\nThis construction never evaluates or compares a quadratic/bilinear exponential\nmoment. It still uses Gaussian auxiliary variables, openly and essentially in\nthe argument supplied here.\n\n## Other apparent replacements and scope failures\n\nUsing the coordinate replacement in the decoupling note would violate this\nban: its conditional comparison is again an MGF-to-Gaussian-MGF match.\n\nA general PSD subgaussian quadratic-form upper-tail theorem also cannot simply\nbe cited as a centered two-sided Hanson–Wright theorem. For example,\n[Hsu–Kakade–Zhang, Theorem 1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1110.2842) controls an upper\ntail of a squared norm using a subgaussian variance proxy. Its trace center need\nnot equal the actual mean, and it does not by itself supply the required lower\ntail. Any attempted use must resolve both gaps and audit the proof dependencies.\n\n## Graph, cost, and conclusion\n\nThe successful branch replaces the Gaussian bilinear-MGF checkpoint with a\nvector-moment checkpoint, then reaches the full moment goal and the theorem.\nIt does not rejoin the original Gaussian MGF state.\n\n**Conclusion:** worked replacement under the stated Gaussian **MGF** comparison\nban. Under a ban on **all** Gaussian comparison/projection/concentration, the\nroute above is inadmissible and no complete replacement is supplied here.\nNeither conclusion is an empirical cost estimate. The distinction belongs in\nthe branch metadata, not only in a footnote to a claimed success.\n"}, "replace": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/09-replace.md", "text": "# Step 9 — `replace`: carry out the Gaussian MGF comparisons\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe reference action conditions on X′ to compare X with g, then on g to compare\nX′ with g′, obtaining an exponential-moment comparison at parameter CK²λ.\n\nUse the same global mechanism ban as [step 8](08-gaussian.md): prohibit Gaussian\ncomparison of quadratic or bilinear MGFs, including repeated scalar-coordinate\nreplacements. Banning only “two replacements” while permitting n versions of the\nsame exponential comparison would fail to remove the underlying strategy.\n\n## Worked alternative and why it qualifies\n\nStart from the theorem, not from an assumed `replaced` fact. Follow the\n[absolute-moment construction](../hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md):\n\n    Q = D + S;\n    even-power coefficient estimates control D;\n    selectors turn S into independent-block bilinear moments;\n    ‖UᵀBY‖p ≤ CK√p ‖BY‖Lp(ℓ₂);\n    ‖BY‖Lp(ℓ₂) ≤ CK(‖B‖F + √p‖B‖).\n\nThe vector step is justified by the projection identity and Gaussian Lipschitz\nestimate written out in step 8. Thus\n\n    ‖S‖p ≤ CK²(√pF+pO),\n    ‖Q‖p ≤ C₀K²(√pF+pO), p even and p ≥ 2.\n\nThe even-order absolute Markov argument M in [the common notes](00-common.md)\nthen closes the full theorem, including small q and the zero cases. No Gaussian\nexponential-moment replacement is used.\n\nThis is explicitly **the same alternative proof** as in step 8, not a second\nindependent discovery. Both graph nodes implement one mathematical strategy\nfamily, even though they play different explanatory roles in the original graph.\n\n## Why a one-sided replacement is not enough\n\nOne may propose replacing only X and leaving X′ unchanged. Under the present\nban that first exponential comparison is already forbidden. Even under a weaker\n“no second replacement” ban, it leaves a random norm involving AX′ whose sharp\ncontrol still needs a proof. Renaming the remaining random norm a deterministic\ncoefficient does not discharge it.\n\nLikewise X and g do not have identical distributions merely because both satisfy\na subgaussian bound. The original operation was an inequality between certain\nexpectations, not equality in law.\n\n## Rejoin, costs, and experiment design\n\nDraw the branch through vector and whole-form moment checkpoints to the same\nfinal theorem; do not attach it to the `replaced` Gaussian-bilinear MGF fact.\nThe extra work is the norm-moment argument and parameter choice. There is no\nmeasured token, call or discovery-time comparison.\n\n**Conclusion:** worked replacement for the Gaussian-MGF mechanism; unresolved\nhere for the broader ban on all Gaussian tools. When reporting criticality,\n`gaussian` and `replace` need a shared strategy-family label. Separate prompt\nvariants can test whether the graph's explanatory role affects a model, but\nshould not be interpreted as two independent mathematical bottlenecks.\n"}, "rotate": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/10-rotate.md", "text": "# Step 10 — `rotate`: use SVD and Gaussian rotational invariance\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe original move turns gᵀAg′ into Σsᵢgᵢgᵢ′ and factors the expectation into\nindependent scalar products.\n\nBan spectral diagonalization of the matrix inside the proof: SVD, an equivalent\neigenvalue reduction of AᵀA or a block matrix, and determinant/log-trace formulas\nwhose use simply hides that reduction. The ban applies in toolkit dependencies\nas well. Basic Euclidean norm identities and scalar Gaussian linear projections\nare allowed; they need not diagonalize A.\n\n## Worked alternative: a norm estimate that preserves the two scales\n\nUse the full absolute-moment route, whose vector-norm checkpoint is\n\n    ‖BY‖Lp(ℓ₂) ≤ CK(‖B‖F + √p‖B‖).\n\nFor a standard Gaussian g, the function f(g) = ‖Bᵀg‖₂ satisfies\n\n    |f(g)−f(h)| ≤ ‖B‖‖g−h‖₂,\n    E f ≤ √(E f²) = √(Σᵢⱼ Bᵢⱼ²) = ‖B‖F.\n\nGaussian Lipschitz concentration therefore gives\n\n    ‖f‖p ≤ ‖B‖F + C√p‖B‖.\n\nIts input is a Lipschitz constant and a mean bound, not a list of singular\nvalues. The second-moment identity uses E gᵢgⱼ = 1{i=j} directly in the existing\ncoordinates. The scalar projection identity\n‖⟨g,v⟩‖p = ‖g₁‖p‖v‖₂ follows from the Gaussian law of a linear combination;\nno rotation that diagonalizes B is required.\n\nThe first-step note uses these facts to close the vector moment checkpoint,\nthen the selector/off-diagonal bound and the full moment estimate. Absolute\nMarkov completes the theorem. Gaussian concentration is an explicit toolkit\ninput; it must be provided independently of Hanson–Wright and of the banned\nmatrix diagonalization, for example via Gaussian isoperimetric concentration.\n\n## A natural bound that is too weak\n\nReplacing the Frobenius calculation by ‖Bᵀg‖₂ ≤ ‖B‖‖g‖₂ gives\n\n    ‖Bᵀg‖p ≤ C‖B‖(√n + √p).\n\nThis can lose √n even for a rank-one B, where the desired leading term is\n‖B‖F = ‖B‖. The successful route uses the mean of the *transformed* norm,\nnot merely the norm of the original Gaussian vector.\n\n## Graph, cost, and conclusion\n\n    no matrix rotation\n      → Gaussian norm Lipschitz constant + coordinate second moment\n      → vector moment estimate → whole-Q moment estimate → theorem\n\nThis bypasses the scalar-product MGF checkpoint and reaches the same final\ngoal. It changes the toolkit burden: spectral factorization is replaced by\nGaussian Lipschitz concentration. The analytic norm scales remain correct;\nrelative search cost is unknown. Calling a determinant formula would not count\nas success under this ban, while the moment route above is a worked replacement.\n"}, "compute": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/11-compute.md", "text": "# Step 11 — `compute`: evaluate the scalar Gaussian-product MGF exactly\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe original action computes E exp(ugg′) = (1−u²)^(−1/2), then bounds it by\nexp(u²) for u² ≤ 1/2.\n\nBan this **exact quadratic/product Gaussian MGF evaluation**, including using\nthe Gaussian-square MGF, a determinant identity, or resumming the Taylor series\nto the same closed form. Permit scalar Gaussian even moments and coefficient\nupper bounds. This removes a particular computation, not every possible way to\nbound the quantity the theorem requires.\n\n## Worked alternative: bound the coefficients without summing the exact MGF\n\nLet W = gg′ with independent standard g,g′. Its law is symmetric. For m ≥ 1,\n\n    E W²ᵐ/(2m)! = ((2m−1)!!)²/(2m)!\n                 = binom(2m,m)/4ᵐ =: c_m.\n\nGaussian even moments follow by integration by parts, without the square MGF.\nNow c₁ = 1/2 and\n\n    c_(m+1)/c_m = (2m+1)/(2m+2) < 1,\n\nso c_m ≤ 1/2. Symmetry and Tonelli applied to the nonnegative cosh series give\n\n    E exp(uW) = E cosh(uW)\n               = 1 + Σₘ≥₁ c_m u²ᵐ\n               ≤ 1 + u²/[2(1−u²)]\n               ≤ 1 + u² ≤ exp(u²),  u² ≤ 1/2.\n\nThe finite geometric upper bound also establishes integrability; no unjustified\nexchange of an alternating or non-integrable exponential series is needed. We\nnever identify the exact sum of the c_m series.\n\nWith the already permitted Gaussian replacement and SVD,\n\n    E exp(θgᵀAg′) = ∏ᵢ E exp(θsᵢgᵢgᵢ′)\n                  ≤ exp(θ²Σᵢsᵢ²) = exp(θ²F²),\n    |θ|O ≤ 1/√2.\n\nThis recovers the exact bound and domain needed by the existing downstream\nassembly. The original proof then closes unchanged after propagating its\nparameter rescaling, tail optimization and final assembly.\n\n## Why truncating the series is not sufficient\n\nWriting E exp(uW) ≈ 1 + u²/2 does not give an upper bound: every omitted even\ncoefficient is positive. The geometric majorant is what controls the entire\nremainder. Keeping a finite number of terms without a remainder estimate would\nleave the proof open.\n\n## Rejoin, cost, and conclusion\n\n    scalar Gaussian moments → coefficient recurrence → geometric majorant\n       → Gaussian-product MGF bound with its domain → original `computed` checkpoint\n\n**Conclusion:** worked replacement with a genuine rejoin at the established\nGaussian MGF checkpoint. It replaces exact evaluation by inequality-level\ncoefficient control. This shows computational flexibility of a calculation;\nit does not identify a new major strategy or measure how difficult either\ncalculation is to discover.\n\nIf the prohibition is expanded to *all* Gaussian quadratic/product MGF bounds,\nthis route no longer qualifies. The moment route in [step 5](05-exponential.md)\nis the appropriate complete bypass for that different ban.\n"}, "meet": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/12-meet.md", "text": "# Step 12 — `meet`: connect the Gaussian bound back to the required MGF\n\n## What exactly can be ablated?\n\nThe original action substitutes θ = CK²λ and chains the decoupling,\nreplacement and Gaussian estimates to discharge the off-diagonal MGF goal.\nIts parameter domain is part of that discharge.\n\nDeleting the displayed edge while permitting the identical chain elsewhere is\nonly a graph-edit experiment. Conversely, banning substitution or transitivity\nof inequalities in every proof would remove basic reasoning, not isolate a\nuseful mathematical strategy. Neither should be assigned a substantive\ncriticality score for Hanson–Wright.\n\nFor a meaningful route-level experiment, forbid **closing the proof through\nthis quadratic-MGF comparison chain**, including packaging it into a lemma or\ninlining the same comparisons directly into a Chernoff bound. Permit a proof\nthat never needs the MGF checkpoint.\n\n## Worked alternative under that route-level ban\n\nFrom the original assumptions, follow the [absolute-moment proof](../hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md).\nIt establishes\n\n    ‖D‖p ≤ CK²(√pF+pO),\n    ‖S‖p ≤ CK²(√pF+pO),\n    ‖Q‖p ≤ C₀K²(√pF+pO), p even and p ≥ 2.\n\nThe two branches are supported by coefficient estimates and selector/vector\nmoments, respectively. Their meeting is Minkowski's inequality at the full\nmoment goal. Apply Markov to |Q|ᵖ with p = 2⌊αq/2⌋ when αq ≥ 4, then handle\nsmall q and degenerate inputs as in calculation M of [the shared notes](00-common.md).\nThis is a complete proof of the same theorem; its new meeting is a different\nmathematical obligation.\n\n## An apparent shortcut that is not a valid alternative\n\nIf the Gaussian estimate holds only for |θ| ≤ a/O, substituting θ = bK²λ\nrequires |λ| ≤ a/(bK²O). Keeping the exponent estimate and allowing arbitrary λ\nwould invalidate the large-deviation regime. Such a missing domain cannot be\nclassified as an “easier alternative proof.”\n\nInlining the correct parameter substitution into `optimize` is valid mathematics,\nbut it still performs the banned comparison-chain assembly. It is evidence that\nthe `meet` node can be omitted from a *drawing*, not evidence that its underlying\nproof route was avoided.\n\n## Graph, cost, and conclusion\n\nReplace the quadratic-MGF meeting with\n\n    independently established diagonal/off-diagonal moments\n       → full absolute-moment goal discharged → absolute tail theorem.\n\nDo not rejoin at `mgf`: that statement has not been established by this branch.\n**Conclusion:** a worked full-route bypass exists under the route-level ban.\nA pure “no assembly/transitivity” ban is not a meaningful step ablation. This\nexperiment overlaps strongly with `exponential`, `gaussian` and `replace`; the\nsame moment proof must not be counted as multiple independent discoveries.\nNo empirical cost or intrinsic difficulty ranking is supplied.\n"}, "optimize": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/13-optimize.md", "text": "# Step 13 — `optimize`: balance a Chernoff parameter against its cutoff\n\n## Original move and global restriction\n\nThe original route selects λ as the smaller of a quadratic-balancing scale and\nan allowed MGF scale. This yields the two terms in min(t²/V,t/L).\n\nBan **threshold-dependent tuning of an exponential/Chernoff parameter** anywhere\nin the alternative proof. This includes rewriting the minimum as a comparable\nsmooth formula, hiding the same choice in an optimization theorem, or calling a\nsubgamma tail lemma that performs it internally. Permit choosing an absolute\nmoment order; that is a different transform route, although the two choices\nserve related mathematical purposes.\n\n## Worked alternative: optimize an even moment instead\n\nProve the whole-form even-moment estimate without quadratic MGFs, as in the\n[first-step construction](../hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md):\n\n    ‖Q‖p ≤ C₀K²(√pF+pO), p even and p ≥ 2.\n\nLet q = min{t²/(K⁴F²), t/(K²O)} and choose a universal α with\nC₀(√α+α) ≤ 1/e. For αq ≥ 4 set p = 2⌊αq/2⌋. Then\n\n    αq/2 ≤ p ≤ αq,\n    C₀K²(√pF+pO)/t ≤ C₀(√α+α) ≤ 1/e,\n    P(|Q| ≥ t) ≤ e⁻ᵖ ≤ e^(−αq/2).\n\nFor αq < 4 use P ≤ 1 and c ≤ α log(2)/4. This proves the theorem without\nselecting λ in any quadratic Chernoff argument. The zero cases are as in\n[the common notes](00-common.md).\n\nThis replacement still makes a scale-sensitive choice: p depends on t and on\nboth matrix norms. It is not a demonstration that scale selection itself can\nbe removed. A global prohibition on all such choices would invalidate this\nalternative and is unresolved here.\n\n## Natural attempts that do not count\n\n1. Set λ to a fixed positive fraction of 1/L. The Chernoff exponent\n   −λt/2 + CVλ² may be positive at moderate or small t; this fixed choice does\n   not give the uniform quadratic regime when V/L² is large.\n2. Use only the balancing choice λ proportional to t/V. At large t it leaves\n   the proved MGF domain. An unavailable MGF is not an expensive calculation;\n   the inference is unsupported.\n3. Choose λ = c t/(V+Lt), with constants adjusted appropriately. This can be a\n   valid bound, but λ is comparable to min(t/V,1/L), so it retains the banned\n   threshold-dependent Chernoff mechanism.\n\n## Graph, cost, and conclusion\n\nThe successful route bypasses the MGF and `tail` checkpoints, establishes a\nfull moment bound, and then reaches the final two-sided theorem. It is a\nworked replacement under the stated exponential-parameter ban, not under a ban\non every scale-sensitive parameter choice.\n\nBoth routes retain the two target norm scales. Their similarity in analytic\nform is not a measured equality of discovery cost. Reusing the existing moment\nproof here is a dependency reuse, not another independently sampled success.\n"}, "finish": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablations/14-finish.md", "text": "# Step 14 — `finish`: assemble four tails and absorb the prefactor\n\n## Define a strategy ban, not a prohibition on finishing a proof\n\nThe original endpoint combines diagonal and off-diagonal upper tails, repeats\nfor −A, and absorbs a prefactor 4 into a weaker exponent to obtain prefactor 2.\n\nBan this **four-tail D/S/sign union and prefactor-absorption route** throughout\nthe alternative proof. Basic logical assembly remains allowed. If “finish” means\nany final inference establishing the theorem, prohibiting it makes the task\nself-defeating rather than identifying a mathematical bottleneck.\n\nThe first-step moment proof also uses the trivial probability bound for small q;\nthat is legitimate under the four-tail ban. If the intended ban instead forbids\nall prefactor absorption or small-q patching, the alternative below reaches\nprefactor 2 directly and makes the narrower boundary clear.\n\n## Worked alternative: one whole-form MGF, then two signs\n\nStart from the theorem, prove the diagonal MGF E and the permitted off-diagonal\nMGF, then apply the Schwarz assembly H in [the common notes](00-common.md):\n\n    E exp(λQ) ≤ exp(C_*Vλ²), |λ| ≤ a_*/L.\n\nFor t > 0 choose λ = min{t/(2C_*V), a_*/(2L)}. Applying exponential Markov\nonce to Q and once to −Q yields\n\n    P(Q ≥ t) ≤ exp(−λt + C_*Vλ²) ≤ exp(−λt/2),\n    P(−Q ≥ t) ≤ exp(−λt + C_*Vλ²) ≤ exp(−λt/2).\n\nSince λt/2 ≥ c min(t²/V,t/L), their union gives\n\n    P(|Q| ≥ t) ≤ 2 exp[−c min(t²/V,t/L)].\n\nThere are exactly two probability terms. D and S were combined at the MGF level,\nso no factor 4 was introduced and no exponent change is needed to absorb it.\nAt t = 0 use probability ≤ 1; zero matrix or zero K are immediate as usual.\nThose elementary boundary cases do not hide an unwanted factor-4 argument.\n\n## Invalid shortcuts and a broader alternative\n\nSimply replacing 4e^(−cq) by 2e^(−cq) at the end of the old proof is invalid:\nthe latter is smaller, and the old upper bound does not imply it. The successful\nroute changes what is assembled *before* taking probabilities.\n\nIf the ban includes **all** positive/negative tail unions, use the complete\nabsolute-moment route from the [first-step note](../hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md)\ninstead. It applies Markov directly to |Q|ᵖ. That broader alternative has its own\nsmall-q handling and cannot be described as avoiding every constant adjustment.\n\n## Graph, cost, and conclusion\n\n    diagonal MGF + off-diagonal MGF\n      → whole-Q MGF via Schwarz → two direct tails → common endpoint.\n\nThis changes upstream proof obligations and is not a replacement that can simply\nbe attached to the old `finish` input state: the old input state contains tail\nbounds, not necessarily the two-sided MGFs needed here. In a global ablation the\nnew branch must include those derivations.\n\n**Conclusion:** worked replacement for the particular four-tail assembly.\nDeleting every possible final inference is not a meaningful strategy ban.\nThe exact prefactor 2 is accounted for, but no relative search cost or\ncriticality score is inferred from that bookkeeping improvement.\n"}, "index": {"path": "hanson_wright_ablation_notes.md", "text": "# Hanson–Wright: ablation notes for every reference move\n\nThis index covers the 14 actions in `tools/proof_explorer/proof.js`: the existing\nfirst-step note and 13 new companion notes. Each companion specifies a global\nstrategy restriction, analyzes a replacement or obstruction, identifies where a\nbranch could legitimately rejoin, and separates mathematical work from unknown\nsearch cost.\n\nThese are **worked mathematical notes, not experimental ablation results**.\nNo new API trials, empirical difficulty estimates, formal proof checks, or\nhistorical frequency counts are reported. A failed candidate is not evidence of\ninfinite search difficulty, and a worked route is not evidence that it can be\ndiscovered at comparable cost to the original.\n\nRead the [shared conventions and calculations](hanson_wright_ablations/00-common.md)\nfirst. They specify the theorem, allowed uses of existing estimates, constants,\nparameter domains, boundary cases, and the meaning of each status below.\n\n## Coverage and findings\n\n“Worked” always means **under the stated ban and available toolkit**, not under\nevery broader interpretation of a step's title.\n\n| Step | Original action | Restriction analyzed | Main alternative and status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [`sign`](hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md) | Upper-tail/sign-reversal strategy | Absolute moments and direct Markov — existing worked route. |\n| 2 | [`expand`](hanson_wright_ablations/02-expand.md) | Any diagonal/off-diagonal separation | Whole-form polarization gives a coarse bound; sharp closure remains unresolved. |\n| 3 | [`split`](hanson_wright_ablations/03-split.md) | Splitting the D/S tail event and assigning thresholds | Combine MGFs with Schwarz before taking tails — worked. The stronger structural ban is step 2. |\n| 4 | [`bernstein`](hanson_wright_ablations/04-bernstein.md) | Diagonal Bernstein/exponential-sum mechanism | Even-power coefficient estimate, then whole-form moments — worked. |\n| 5 | [`exponential`](hanson_wright_ablations/05-exponential.md) | Quadratic/bilinear MGF and Chernoff route | Absolute moments — worked. |\n| 6 | [`linear`](hanson_wright_ablations/06-linear.md) | Non-Gaussian linear-MGF factorization/comparison | Symmetrization and termwise Gaussian even-moment comparison — worked. |\n| 7 | [`decouple`](hanson_wright_ablations/07-decouple.md) | Independent-copy, selector, and equivalent block decoupling | Replace coordinates individually in a hollow form — worked, source-assisted. |\n| 8 | [`gaussian`](hanson_wright_ablations/08-gaussian.md) | Gaussian quadratic/bilinear MGF comparison | Gaussian projection and norm moments — worked; a ban on all Gaussian tools remains unresolved here. |\n| 9 | [`replace`](hanson_wright_ablations/09-replace.md) | Same Gaussian MGF-comparison family as step 8 | Same moment-route bypass — worked; not a separate discovery. |\n| 10 | [`rotate`](hanson_wright_ablations/10-rotate.md) | Matrix SVD/eigenvalue reduction and disguised spectral formulas | Gaussian Lipschitz norm control plus coordinate second moments — worked. |\n| 11 | [`compute`](hanson_wright_ablations/11-compute.md) | Exact Gaussian-product/square MGF evaluation | Bound the even-moment coefficients geometrically — worked, with the original domain retained. |\n| 12 | [`meet`](hanson_wright_ablations/12-meet.md) | Closing through the original quadratic-MGF comparison chain | Meet at a full absolute-moment goal — worked. Banning basic transitivity is not a useful experiment. |\n| 13 | [`optimize`](hanson_wright_ablations/13-optimize.md) | Threshold-dependent Chernoff-parameter tuning | Choose an even absolute-moment order — worked; scale-sensitive parameter choice remains. |\n| 14 | [`finish`](hanson_wright_ablations/14-finish.md) | Four-tail D/S/sign union and prefactor absorption | Whole-Q MGF followed by two tails — worked. Banning every final inference is self-defeating. |\n\n## How these notes should become graph branches\n\nA branch must contain the intervening mathematical work, not just an “alternative\nmethod” label. In particular:\n\n- The moment routes replace MGF or tail checkpoints with new moment obligations.\n  They usually reach the final theorem rather than recreating the removed state.\n- The scalar coefficient alternative in step 11 recovers the original Gaussian\n  MGF bound **and its parameter domain**, so it supports a genuine rejoin.\n- The coordinate-replacement route in step 7 keeps a coupled Gaussian quadratic\n  form; it does not recreate the old independent-copy bilinear fact.\n- Step 2 has no demonstrated closure. Its current candidate yields a weaker bound;\n  mark that candidate as insufficient and retain the broader question as unresolved.\n- Equal final theorems do not imply equal intermediate states or proof histories.\n  Align endpoints without merging incompatible facts.\n\nGreen-grey may indicate a **worked mathematical success**, provided its evidence\nlabel remains visible. It must not mean “verified by a theorem prover” or “found\nwithin a measured finite search budget.” An unsuccessful or incomplete analytic\ncandidate should carry its actual reason; none of these notes justifies an\ninfinite-difficulty label.\n\nThe explorer now attaches these notes and curated derivation maps to all 14\nreference actions. Click a step to reveal its branches, and click it again to\nrestore the original graph. Most maps explicitly replan from the assumptions;\nonly step 11 recovers the original checkpoint and parameter domain for a rejoin.\nThe maps summarize written arguments; they are not newly executed searches or\nformal state-transition certificates.\n\n## What can be concluded about criticality?\n\nSeveral individual moves can be bypassed mathematically, but much of that\ncoverage comes from **one shared absolute-moment route**. It is not a collection\nof independent successful searches. That route itself needs diagonal/off-diagonal\nseparation, decoupling, sharp linear moments, and Gaussian norm tools.\n\nThe coordinate-replacement proof makes decoupling replaceable when Gaussian MGF\ncomparison is allowed; the moment route makes Gaussian MGF comparison replaceable\nwhen selector decoupling is allowed. Neither fact proves that both strategies can\nbe removed simultaneously. Their joint ablation is a separate unresolved task in\nthese notes, not an inferred impossibility.\n\nThe `gaussian`/`replace` pair is one strategy family represented at two locations.\nThe `meet` and `finish` actions also include basic assembly. Rankings based solely\non individual graph-node removal can therefore mix mathematical strategies,\nrepresentation choices, and bookkeeping.\n\nA later empirical study should freeze each semantic ban and its toolkit, use\nindependent baseline/restricted attempts under the same budgets, audit disguised\nreuse, and record discovery cost separately from review cost. The first-step\npilot design can be adapted per strategy family; these documents neither run\nnor authorize that experiment. Budget exhaustion should remain censored, not\nbe assigned infinite cost.\n\n## Sources and provenance\n\nThe source-assisted coordinate replacement is attributed within [step 7](hanson_wright_ablations/07-decouple.md),\nincluding the adaptation from its stated distributional setting. The PSD\nupper-tail comparison discussed in [step 8](hanson_wright_ablations/08-gaussian.md)\nis cited there with the scope limitation that prevents treating it as the target\ntheorem. Other alternatives are local derivations or explicit reuse of the\n[first-step mathematical reconstruction](hanson_wright_sign_ablation.md).\n\nIn particular, step 7 was found online in Ingvar Ziemann's\n[An Elementary Proof of The Hanson-Wright Inequality (2025), §2](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.00881v1#S2).\nIts success records a literature-assisted replacement, not independent agent\ndiscovery or measured ease of search. It therefore does not by itself show that\ndecoupling is noncritical to discovering the original proof. See the step's\nprovenance discussion for the distinction between retrieval, adaptation,\nverification, and discovery cost.\n"}};if(typeof module==="object"&&module.exports)module.exports=notes;else root.HansonWrightAblationNotes=notes;})(typeof window==="undefined"?globalThis:window);
